# Phase M2 — Project foundation

**Status:** In progress (M2.1, M2.6, M2.7 done) · **Tasks:** TASKS.md M2.1–M2.16 · **Depends on:** M0 (repo, PR workflow), M1 (architecture.md module list, api-design.md error model) · **Date:** 2026-11-01

This phase builds the scaffolding every feature will stand on: the database and object storage running locally, configuration per environment, module boundaries enforced by tests, one consistent error format, request IDs in every log line, formatting and coverage checks, and CI that runs it all on every pull request. No business feature ships in M2. What ships is the guarantee that when features arrive in M3–M8, they're built, tested and checked the same way every time.

---

## 1. Goal

At the end of this phase:

- `docker compose up -d` starts PostgreSQL 16 and MinIO with health checks and persistent volumes.
- The application starts with `local`, `test` or `prod` configuration, and as an `api` node, a `worker` node, or both.
- Flyway applies `V1__initial_schema.sql`, `V2__auth_hardening.sql` and `V3__refresh_token_constraints.sql`; Hibernate validates the entities against them.
- Module packages exist and a test fails the build if one module reaches into another's internals.
- Every error response is an RFC 9457 `ProblemDetail` with a `code` and `requestId`.
- Every request has an `X-Request-Id`, echoed in the response and present in every log line; `prod` logs are JSON.
- `./mvnw verify` runs unit tests, Testcontainers integration tests, the formatting check and a JaCoCo coverage report.
- GitHub Actions runs `./mvnw verify` on every PR, and `main` requires it to pass.

**The demo that proves it:**

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps                         # postgres and minio: "healthy"
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local,api,worker ./mvnw spring-boot:run
curl -s localhost:8080/actuator/health    # {"status":"UP", ...}
curl -si localhost:8080/api/v1/nope | head -20
#   HTTP/1.1 401 (or 404 once auth is in) with Content-Type: application/problem+json
#   and an X-Request-Id header
./mvnw verify                             # green, including Testcontainers tests
```

and a PR on GitHub shows a green `build` check that's required for merging.

---

## 2. Concepts to understand first

### 2.1 Spring Boot auto-configuration and Boot 4's modular starters

Spring Boot looks at the classpath and the configuration and creates beans for you: put a JDBC driver and `spring.datasource.url` on the classpath and you get a `DataSource`. In **Boot 4**, auto-configuration was split into many small modules, and each technology has its own starter. That's why `flyway-core` alone did nothing in this project: the `FlywayAutoConfiguration` lives in `spring-boot-flyway`, which `spring-boot-starter-flyway` brings in (the comment in `pom.xml` records this). Expect the same pattern for every technology in this phase: add the **starter**, not just the library. When unsure of a name, check the Boot 4 reference documentation's starter list.

### 2.2 Schema ownership: Flyway versus Hibernate

Two tools could create tables: Flyway (versioned SQL scripts) and Hibernate (`ddl-auto=create/update`). Exactly one must own the schema. Here **Flyway owns it**: the SQL is reviewed, versioned, repeatable and supports partitioning, partial indexes and triggers that Hibernate can't generate. Hibernate runs with `ddl-auto=validate`: at startup it compares each entity with the table and **fails the boot** on a mismatch, which catches mapping bugs before any request does.

**Interview angle:** "Why not `ddl-auto=update` in production?" It can't drop or rename, it can't express constraints like partial unique indexes, it isn't reviewed, and it can make different changes on different environments.

### 2.3 Open Session in View

`spring.jpa.open-in-view=true` (Spring's default) keeps the Hibernate session open for the whole HTTP request, including JSON serialization. Lazy associations then load silently during serialization: N+1 queries outside any transaction, holding a DB connection for the full request. With it **off** (already set), lazy loading outside a transaction throws `LazyInitializationException`, which forces you to fetch exactly what each endpoint needs inside the service transaction. Loud failures beat silent slowness.

### 2.4 Testcontainers and `@ServiceConnection`

**Testcontainers** starts real dependencies (PostgreSQL 16, MinIO) in Docker for the duration of the tests. You test against the exact database engine you deploy, including partitioning, `SKIP LOCKED` and RLS, which H2 or an embedded database can't emulate. `@ServiceConnection` on a container bean tells Spring Boot to derive the connection details (URL, username, password) from the running container, replacing the `spring.datasource.*` properties.

**Interview angle:** "Why not H2 for tests?" Because H2 isn't PostgreSQL. Partial indexes, `NULLS NOT DISTINCT`, JSONB, partitioning, row-level security and `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` would either fail or behave differently, so the tests would pass while production breaks.

### 2.5 Configuration profiles

A **profile** is a named set of configuration activated at startup (`SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod`). `application.yml` holds the defaults; `application-<profile>.yml` overrides them. This project uses two independent dimensions:

| Dimension | Profiles | Chooses |
|---|---|---|
| Environment | `local`, `test`, `prod` | Where it runs: logging format, secrets source, dev conveniences |
| Role | `api`, `worker` | What it does: serve HTTP traffic, process background jobs, or both (local runs both) |

Combine them: `prod,api` on the API nodes, `prod,worker` on the worker nodes, `local,api,worker` on your laptop. This is decision D1 in `docs/scalability.md` §3.1 (folded in here from scalability.md §9).

**Rule:** secrets never live in committed files. Committed YAML references `${ENV_VAR}`; values come from the environment (or the gitignored `.env` locally).

### 2.6 Module boundaries and Spring Modulith

In a modular monolith (ADR 0002), each top-level package under `com.reconflow.paymentreconflow` is a **module**. **Spring Modulith** treats a module's top-level package as its public API and every sub-package as internal. `ApplicationModules.of(App.class).verify()` fails if module A uses an internal type of module B, or if modules form a cycle. A sub-package can be opened deliberately with `@NamedInterface`. That turns the dependency rules written in `docs/architecture.md` §4 into a test that breaks the build.

### 2.7 RFC 9457 Problem Details

A standard JSON shape for HTTP errors (`application/problem+json`) with `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`, plus any extension members. Spring has built-in support (`ProblemDetail`, `ErrorResponse`, `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler`). One consistent error shape lets clients handle errors generically, and the `code` member gives them a stable machine-readable value that doesn't change when the human-readable `title` is reworded.

### 2.8 Correlation IDs and MDC

A **request ID** identifies one HTTP request across every log line it produces, and the client gets it back in a response header, so "it failed" becomes "request `4f1c2a…` failed" and support can find the logs. **MDC** (Mapped Diagnostic Context) is a per-thread map that the logging framework adds to every log line written on that thread. A servlet filter puts the ID in the MDC at the start of the request and removes it at the end.

**Interview angle:** MDC is thread-local, so it doesn't follow work handed to another thread (`@Async`, executors, the job worker). Copy it explicitly, or put the originating request ID in the job payload.

### 2.9 Quality gates and CI

A **quality gate** is an automated check that must pass before merging: compile, tests, formatting, (later) coverage thresholds and vulnerability scans. **CI** runs them on a clean machine for every PR, and branch protection (M0.4) makes passing mandatory. Formatting is automated (Spotless) so code review discusses design, never whitespace.

