## 4. Step by step

Order: infrastructure first (so tests can run), then configuration, then code structure, then quality gates and CI. Each numbered task is one PR unless noted.

### 4.1 M2.1 — Data dependencies (done)

**What:** JPA, PostgreSQL driver, Flyway.

**Already in place:** `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa`, `postgresql` (runtime), `spring-boot-starter-flyway`, `flyway-database-postgresql` (Flyway 10+ moved each database's support into its own module; without it Flyway fails with "Unsupported Database: PostgreSQL 16").

**Done when:** already true. Tick it, with a note that the starter replaces `flyway-core`.

### 4.2 M2.4 — `compose.yaml` with PostgreSQL and MinIO

**What:** one file that starts every local dependency.

**Why:** a new contributor runs one command instead of installing PostgreSQL and configuring `pg_hba.conf` (the README's current setup). MinIO is added here because `FileStorage` is S3-compatible from M5 (scalability.md §9, M2.4 change).

**How:** create `compose.yaml` at the root:

```yaml
name: reconflow

services:
  postgres:
    # Pin the minor version: "postgres:16" floats and can change under you.
    image: postgres:16.4-alpine
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: paymentreconflow
      POSTGRES_USER: ${DB_USER:?set DB_USER in .env}
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:?set DB_PASSWORD in .env}
      # Require passwords even for local connections (see README "Local auth").
      POSTGRES_INITDB_ARGS: "--auth-host=scram-sha-256 --auth-local=scram-sha-256"
    ports:
      - "5432:5432"
    volumes:
      - postgres-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U $${POSTGRES_USER} -d $${POSTGRES_DB}"]
      interval: 5s
      timeout: 3s
      retries: 10

  minio:
    image: minio/minio:RELEASE.2025-09-07T16-13-09Z   # pin a release tag; check Docker Hub for the latest
    command: server /data --console-address ":9001"
    environment:
      MINIO_ROOT_USER: ${S3_ACCESS_KEY:?set S3_ACCESS_KEY in .env}
      MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD: ${S3_SECRET_KEY:?set S3_SECRET_KEY in .env}
    ports:
      - "9000:9000"   # S3 API
      - "9001:9001"   # web console
    volumes:
      - minio-data:/data
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD", "mc", "ready", "local"]
      interval: 5s
      timeout: 3s
      retries: 10

  # One-shot job: creates the bucket once MinIO is healthy, then exits.
  minio-init:
    image: minio/mc:latest
    depends_on:
      minio:
        condition: service_healthy
    entrypoint: >
      /bin/sh -c "
      mc alias set local http://minio:9000 $${S3_ACCESS_KEY} $${S3_SECRET_KEY} &&
      mc mb --ignore-existing local/$${S3_BUCKET}
      "
    environment:
      S3_ACCESS_KEY: ${S3_ACCESS_KEY}
      S3_SECRET_KEY: ${S3_SECRET_KEY}
      S3_BUCKET: ${S3_BUCKET:-reconflow-imports}

volumes:
  postgres-data:
  minio-data:
```

Docker Compose reads `.env` in the project directory automatically, the same file the app imports. Add to `.env.example`:

```properties
# Object storage (MinIO locally, S3 in production)
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_ACCESS_KEY=reconflow
S3_SECRET_KEY=change-me-at-least-8-chars
S3_BUCKET=reconflow-imports
S3_REGION=us-east-1
```

**Pitfalls:**

- `$${VAR}` (double dollar) inside `command`/`entrypoint`/`healthcheck` passes a literal `$` to the container shell; a single `$` is interpolated by Compose from `.env` at parse time. Both work here, but know the difference.
- **Named volumes** survive `docker compose down`. To wipe the database (e.g. after editing a migration during experiments, which you should never do to an applied one), run `docker compose down -v`.
- `depends_on` with `condition: service_healthy` waits for the health check, not just for the container to start. Without the health check, the init job races MinIO.
- `POSTGRES_*` environment variables only take effect on the **first** start with an empty volume. Changing the password later requires `down -v` or `ALTER ROLE`.
- If you already run a Homebrew PostgreSQL on 5432, one of them fails to bind. Stop the Homebrew service (`brew services stop postgresql@16`) or map `5433:5432` and change `DB_URL`.

**Optional:** Boot's `spring-boot-docker-compose` module can start `compose.yaml` automatically on `spring-boot:run`. It's convenient, but keep the explicit `docker compose up` in the README so the dependency is visible. Verify the Boot 4 artifact name if you add it.

**Done when:** `docker compose up -d && docker compose ps` shows `postgres` and `minio` as `healthy`, `minio-init` exited with code 0, and `http://localhost:9001` shows the bucket.

### 4.3 M2.3 — Testcontainers

**What:** integration tests start their own PostgreSQL (and later MinIO) containers.

**Why:** see §2.4. Also fixes the fresh-clone failure (M0 exit criterion).

**How:** add to `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-testcontainers</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
    <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
    <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
<!-- Added in M5 when FileStorage lands:
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
    <artifactId>testcontainers-minio</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
-->
```

Testcontainers 2.x renamed its modules (`postgresql` → `testcontainers-postgresql`) and moved container classes into per-module packages (`org.testcontainers.postgresql.PostgreSQLContainer`). Boot 4's dependency management pins the version, so no `<version>` tags. **Verify the exact artifact IDs and package names against the Boot 4 dependency versions page**; if Boot 4.1 still manages Testcontainers 1.x, the artifacts are `org.testcontainers:postgresql` and `org.testcontainers:junit-jupiter`.

Create `src/test/java/com/reconflow/paymentreconflow/TestcontainersConfiguration.java`:

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow;

import org.springframework.boot.test.context.TestConfiguration;
import org.springframework.boot.testcontainers.service.connection.ServiceConnection;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.testcontainers.postgresql.PostgreSQLContainer;
import org.testcontainers.utility.DockerImageName;

@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class TestcontainersConfiguration {

    // Same image as compose.yaml, so tests run on the engine you develop against.
    static final DockerImageName POSTGRES = DockerImageName.parse("postgres:16.4-alpine");

    @Bean
    @ServiceConnection
    PostgreSQLContainer postgres() {
        return new PostgreSQLContainer(POSTGRES);
    }
}
```

You can also run the app locally *against* Testcontainers, with no compose, through a test-scoped main class. Boot's "development-time services" feature:

```java
// src/test/java/com/reconflow/paymentreconflow/TestPaymentreconflowApplication.java
public class TestPaymentreconflowApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.from(PaymentreconflowApplication::main)
                .with(TestcontainersConfiguration.class)
                .run(args);
    }
}
```

Run with `./mvnw spring-boot:test-run`.

**Pitfalls:**

- Docker must be running. On macOS with Docker Desktop that's automatic; with Colima, export `DOCKER_HOST` and `TESTCONTAINERS_DOCKER_SOCKET_OVERRIDE=/var/run/docker.sock` (see the Testcontainers docs for Colima).
- Every distinct Spring test context starts its own container. Different `@MockitoBean` sets or `@ActiveProfiles` per test class create different contexts. The base class in M2.16 keeps one shared context.
- `${DB_USER}` with no default in `application.yml`: if startup complains it can't resolve the placeholder, the `test` profile (M2.5) supplies harmless defaults.

**Done when:** with no `.env` and no local PostgreSQL, `./mvnw test` passes and logs `Successfully applied 3 migrations to schema "public"`.

### 4.4 M2.5 — `application.yml` profiles

**What:** split configuration into defaults plus environment and role overlays.

**Why:** see §2.5.

**How:** keep `src/main/resources/application.yml` for shared defaults, and add overlays.

`application.yml` (shared; changes from today marked):

```yaml
spring:
  application:
    name: reconflow
  config:
    import: optional:file:.env[.properties]
  datasource:
    url: ${DB_URL:jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/paymentreconflow}
    username: ${DB_USER:}          # empty default: the test profile and @ServiceConnection don't need it
    password: ${DB_PASSWORD:}
    hikari:
      maximum-pool-size: ${DB_POOL_SIZE:10}
      connection-timeout: 5000
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: validate
    open-in-view: false
    properties:
      hibernate:
        jdbc:
          time_zone: UTC
  flyway:
    enabled: true
    locations: classpath:db/migration
    baseline-on-migrate: false
  mvc:
    problemdetails:
      enabled: true               # NEW: Spring MVC's own exceptions render as ProblemDetail
  servlet:
    multipart:
      max-file-size: 50MB         # NEW: CSV contract limit (M1.3); enforced again in code (M10.3)
      max-request-size: 51MB

server:
  port: ${PORT:8080}
  shutdown: graceful              # NEW: finish in-flight requests on SIGTERM

management:
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health,info
  endpoint:
    health:
      show-details: when-authorized
      probes:
        enabled: true             # NEW: /actuator/health/liveness and /readiness (M9.11 builds on it)

reconflow:
  storage:
    endpoint: ${S3_ENDPOINT:}
    region: ${S3_REGION:us-east-1}
    bucket: ${S3_BUCKET:reconflow-imports}
    access-key: ${S3_ACCESS_KEY:}
    secret-key: ${S3_SECRET_KEY:}
  jobs:
    worker:
      enabled: false              # the worker profile turns it on

logging:
  level:
    org.flywaydb: INFO
```

`application-local.yml`:

```yaml
logging:
  level:
    com.reconflow: DEBUG
    org.hibernate.SQL: DEBUG     # see every SQL statement while developing
management:
  endpoint:
    health:
      show-details: always
```

`src/test/resources/application-test.yml` (in **test** resources, so it never ships):

```yaml
spring:
  datasource:
    username: test   # overridden by @ServiceConnection; present so placeholders resolve
    password: test
logging:
  level:
    org.testcontainers: INFO
    com.github.dockerjava: WARN
```

`application-prod.yml`:

```yaml
spring:
  datasource:
    url: ${DB_URL}           # no defaults in prod: missing config fails at startup
    username: ${DB_USER}
    password: ${DB_PASSWORD}
  config:
    import: ""               # never read a .env file in production
logging:
  structured:
    format:
      console: ecs           # M2.12
server:
  error:
    include-stacktrace: never
```

`application-api.yml` and `application-worker.yml`:

```yaml
# application-worker.yml
reconflow:
  jobs:
    worker:
      enabled: true
      threads: 4
```

```yaml
# application-api.yml
# Nothing yet: API beans are on by default. Exists so the profile is explicit
# and has a home for API-only settings (rate limits in M10.4).
```

**Decision: role switching uses a property, not `@Profile` annotations.** The job worker bean is created with `@ConditionalOnBooleanProperty("reconflow.jobs.worker.enabled")` (verify the annotation name in Boot 4; `@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "...", havingValue = "true")` works everywhere). The `worker` profile sets the property. That keeps the profile names in YAML only, where you can see them, rather than scattered across `@Profile("worker")` annotations. API nodes have the worker disabled; worker nodes still start the web server so `/actuator/health` works for the orchestrator, but the load balancer only routes to API nodes.

Bind the `reconflow.*` properties to a typed record in `shared`:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties("reconflow.storage")
@Validated
public record StorageProperties(String endpoint, @NotBlank String region, @NotBlank String bucket,
                                String accessKey, String secretKey) {}
```

and enable it with `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` on the application class.

**How to activate profiles:**

| Where | How |
|---|---|
| Laptop | `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local,api,worker ./mvnw spring-boot:run`, or `-Dspring-boot.run.profiles=local,api,worker` |
| Tests | `@ActiveProfiles("test")` on the base class (M2.16) |
| Production | `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod,api` / `prod,worker` set by the platform |

**Pitfalls:**

- Don't set `spring.profiles.active` inside a profile-specific file; Boot rejects it. Activate profiles from outside.
- **No default profile that's convenient for development.** If `local` were the default, a production deploy that forgot `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE` would run with DEBUG SQL logging and generated JWT keys (authentication.plan §7). With no default, it runs with the strict base config.
- `${DB_USER:}` (empty default) in the base file plus `${DB_USER}` (no default) in `prod` means: lenient in tests, fail-fast in production.

**Done when:** `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local,api,worker ./mvnw spring-boot:run` starts against compose; `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod,api` with `DB_URL` unset fails immediately with a clear "Could not resolve placeholder 'DB_URL'".

### 4.5 M2.6 / M2.7 — `ddl-auto=validate` and `open-in-view=false` (done)

Both are already in `application.yml`. See §2.2 and §2.3 for the reasons, which you should be able to explain. Tick them.

**One check to add:** a test that the settings stay that way, because someone (you, in six months) might flip them while debugging:

```java
class JpaSettingsTest extends AbstractIntegrationTest {
    @Value("${spring.jpa.open-in-view}") boolean openInView;
    @Value("${spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto}") String ddlAuto;

    @Test
    void schemaIsOwnedByFlywayAndSessionsDoNotLeakIntoViews() {
        assertThat(openInView).isFalse();
        assertThat(ddlAuto).isEqualTo("validate");
    }
}
```

### 4.6 M2.2 — Actuator and Security

**What:** actuator is already present. Add `spring-boot-starter-security`.

**Why now:** so every endpoint written from M3 onward is secured by default, rather than retrofitting security onto open endpoints.

**How:**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security-test</artifactId>  <!-- verify the Boot 4 test starter name; else spring-security-test -->
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

**Warning — what happens the moment you add it:** Spring Boot's default security locks **every** endpoint, including `/actuator/health`, behind HTTP Basic with a generated user (`user` / a password printed in the log). Health checks from Docker, the load balancer and CI then get `401`. Until M3.3 builds real authentication, add a temporary filter chain:

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.security.config;

// TEMPORARY (M2.2): replaced by the real chain in M3.3 (authentication.plan §6).
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())          // stateless API, no cookies yet
            .httpBasic(basic -> basic.disable())
            .formLogin(form -> form.disable())
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/actuator/health/**", "/actuator/info").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated());    // everything else: 401 until M3
        return http.build();
    }

    // Stops Boot generating and logging a default user.
    @Bean
    UserDetailsService noUsers() {
        return username -> { throw new UsernameNotFoundException(username); };
    }
}
```

`anyRequest().authenticated()` with no login mechanism means every other path returns `401`. That's the correct default: deny until proven allowed. Make the `401` a `ProblemDetail` too, by registering an `AuthenticationEntryPoint` that writes one (M2.10 shows the shape).

**Pitfalls:**

- Don't `permitAll()` everything "for now". It always outlives "for now".
- `show-details: when-authorized` on health means anonymous callers see only `{"status":"UP"}`. Good: component details (DB version, disk) are information an attacker would like.

**Done when:** `curl -i localhost:8080/actuator/health` → `200`; `curl -i localhost:8080/api/v1/anything` → `401` with `application/problem+json`; no "Using generated security password" line in the log.

### 4.7 M2.8 — Module packages

**What:** one package per module, each with a `package-info.java` describing it.

**Why:** the boundaries from `docs/architecture.md` §3 need somewhere to live before code arrives.

**How:**

```
src/main/java/com/reconflow/paymentreconflow/
├── PaymentreconflowApplication.java
├── organization/package-info.java
├── security/package-info.java
├── ingestion/package-info.java
├── ledger/package-info.java
├── reconciliation/package-info.java
├── exceptions/package-info.java
├── reporting/package-info.java
├── audit/package-info.java
└── shared/
    ├── package-info.java
    ├── error/      ProblemCodes, ApiException and subclasses, GlobalExceptionHandler (M2.10)
    ├── web/        RequestIdFilter (M2.11), PageResponse
    ├── id/         Ids (UUIDv7)
    ├── json/       Jackson configuration (money as strings)
    ├── config/     typed @ConfigurationProperties records
    └── job/        job queue (filled in M5)
```

Example `package-info.java`:

```java
/**
 * Ingestion: file upload, storage, parsing, validation and normalization of
 * CSV imports. Owns import_batch and import_row_error.
 * Public API: this package. Everything in sub-packages is internal.
 */
@org.springframework.modulith.ApplicationModule(displayName = "Ingestion")
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.ingestion;
```

**Shared building blocks to add in this task** (small, used everywhere from M3):

*UUIDv7 IDs (ADR 0010).* The JDK has no UUIDv7 generator. Either use a library such as `com.github.f4b6a3:uuid-creator` (`UuidCreator.getTimeOrderedEpoch()`), or Hibernate's `@UuidGenerator(style = UuidGenerator.Style.VERSION_7)` (verify it exists in the Hibernate version Boot 4.1 ships). Prefer one static entry point so the choice lives in one place:

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.shared.id;

public final class Ids {
    private Ids() {}

    /** Time-ordered UUIDv7: unguessable enough to expose, index-friendly to insert. */
    public static UUID newId() {
        return UuidCreator.getTimeOrderedEpoch();
    }
}
```

Assigning IDs in the application (not on insert) means the ID is known before saving, so it can go in the audit event and the `Location` header in the same transaction, and Hibernate can batch inserts.

*Money as JSON strings* (rule from `docs/reconciliation-rules.md` §8). In Boot 4, Jackson 3 lives under `tools.jackson`; register a customizer that serializes every `BigDecimal` as a plain string:

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.shared.json;

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class JsonConfig {
    // Verify the Boot 4 customizer type (JsonMapperBuilderCustomizer) and the
    // Jackson 3 feature names against the docs; the intent is:
    //   BigDecimal -> "1000.0000" (string, plain notation, never 1E+3)
    @Bean
    JsonMapperBuilderCustomizer moneyAsString() {
        return builder -> builder
                .addModule(new SimpleModule().addSerializer(BigDecimal.class, new ToStringSerializer() {
                    @Override
                    public void serialize(Object value, JsonGenerator gen, SerializationContext ctx) {
                        gen.writeString(((BigDecimal) value).toPlainString());
                    }
                }));
    }
}
```

Test it with a `@JsonTest` slice: `{"amount": new BigDecimal("1E+3")}` serializes to `"1000"`.

**Role profiles (scalability.md §9, M2.8 change):** the `api`/`worker` property switch from §4.4 is part of this task's structure: the (future) `JobWorker` in `shared.jobs` is the only bean conditional on `reconflow.jobs.worker.enabled`.

**Pitfalls:**

- Empty packages aren't committed by Git; the `package-info.java` files keep them.
- Don't create `controller/`, `service/`, `repository/` packages at the root ("package by layer"). Package by **feature** (module), then by layer inside if useful.

**Done when:** the tree above exists and the app still starts.

### 4.8 M2.9 — Enforce module boundaries

**What:** a test that fails when a module uses another's internals or modules form a cycle.

**How:** add Spring Modulith (manage its version with its BOM; Modulith 2.x targets Boot 4; verify the exact version):

```xml
<dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-modulith-bom</artifactId>
            <version>2.0.x</version> <!-- the release matching Boot 4.1; check the Modulith compatibility table -->
            <type>pom</type>
            <scope>import</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</dependencyManagement>

<!-- in <dependencies> -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-modulith-starter-core</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-modulith-starter-test</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

`src/test/java/com/reconflow/paymentreconflow/ModularityTest.java`:

```java
class ModularityTest {

    static final ApplicationModules modules = ApplicationModules.of(PaymentreconflowApplication.class);

    @Test
    void modulesRespectTheirBoundaries() {
        modules.verify();   // no cycles, no access to another module's internal packages
    }

    @Test
    void writeDocumentation() {
        // Generates PlantUML/C4 diagrams of the actual module graph into target/spring-modulith-docs.
        // Compare them with docs/architecture.md §4 when reviewing.
        new Documenter(modules).writeDocumentation();
    }
}
```

`verify()` needs no Spring context and no database; it's a fast unit test.

**Declaring the allowed dependencies** from `docs/architecture.md` §4 explicitly, so an *allowed-but-unplanned* dependency also fails:

```java
@ApplicationModule(allowedDependencies = { "ledger", "audit", "shared :: *", "security :: tenant" })
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.ingestion;
```

- `shared` is used by everyone, including its sub-packages. Either mark it `@ApplicationModule(type = ApplicationModule.Type.OPEN)` (all sub-packages public), or expose each sub-package with `@NamedInterface`. Open is simpler and fine for a module of building blocks.
- `security` exposes only a small user-directory API (membership and display-name lookups) as a named interface, e.g. `@NamedInterface("directory")` in `security/directory/package-info.java`. The tenant and identity **interfaces** (`TenantContext`, `CurrentUser`, `TenantScope`, `Permission`) live in `shared` and are implemented inside `security` (phase-M3 explains why: it avoids a `security → audit → security` cycle). Everything else, including `security.auth` and `security.token`, stays internal, which enforces the authentication.plan §6 rule.

**ArchUnit, only for what Modulith can't express.** Add it (`com.tngtech.archunit:archunit-junit5`, test scope) when the first such rule appears. Candidates:

- "No class outside `security` reads `SecurityContextHolder`" (M3.3i).
- "No field or parameter of type `double`/`float` in any class whose name contains `Amount` or in entities" (money rule).
- "Repository methods in tenant-owned modules declare an `organizationId` parameter" (M3.5).

**Pitfalls:** `verify()` on an empty project passes trivially. Prove it works: temporarily make `ledger` import a class from `ingestion.internal`, run the test, see it fail, revert.

**Done when:** `./mvnw test -Dtest=ModularityTest` passes, and the deliberate violation makes it fail.

### 4.9 M2.10 — Global `ProblemDetail` error handling

**What:** one `@RestControllerAdvice` that turns every exception into the error format in `docs/api-design.md` §3.

**Why:** consistent errors for clients; no stack traces or SQL leaking to callers; a single place to log unexpected failures.

**How:** first, a small exception hierarchy in `shared.error`:

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.shared.error;

/** Base for errors the API reports deliberately. code is the stable kebab-case identifier. */
public abstract class ApiException extends RuntimeException {
    private final HttpStatus status;
    private final String code;
    private final Map<String, Object> properties;

    protected ApiException(HttpStatus status, String code, String detail, Map<String, Object> properties) {
        super(detail);
        this.status = status;
        this.code = code;
        this.properties = Map.copyOf(properties);
    }
    // getters
}

public final class NotFoundException extends ApiException {
    public NotFoundException(String entity, UUID id) {
        super(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, "not-found", entity + " " + id + " was not found", Map.of());
    }
}

public final class ConflictException extends ApiException {
    public ConflictException(String code, String detail, Map<String, Object> properties) {
        super(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, code, detail, properties);
    }
}
```

Then the handler. Extending `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` gives ProblemDetail rendering for all of Spring MVC's own exceptions (unsupported media type, missing parameter, malformed JSON, …); you override only where you want different output:

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.shared.error;

@RestControllerAdvice
class GlobalExceptionHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(GlobalExceptionHandler.class);
    static final String TYPE_BASE = "https://reconflow.dev/problems/";

    @ExceptionHandler(ApiException.class)
    ProblemDetail handleApi(ApiException ex) {
        ProblemDetail pd = problem(ex.getStatus(), ex.getCode(), ex.getMessage());
        ex.getProperties().forEach(pd::setProperty);
        return pd;
    }

    // Bean Validation on @RequestBody
    @Override
    protected ResponseEntity<Object> handleMethodArgumentNotValid(
            MethodArgumentNotValidException ex, HttpHeaders headers, HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {
        ProblemDetail pd = problem(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, "validation-failed", "The request is invalid.");
        pd.setProperty("errors", ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
                .map(e -> Map.of("field", e.getField(),
                                 "code", String.valueOf(e.getCode()),
                                 "message", String.valueOf(e.getDefaultMessage())))
                .toList());
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(pd);
    }

    // Optimistic locking failure that escaped the service layer (M7.8 maps it explicitly too)
    @ExceptionHandler(ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException.class)
    ProblemDetail handleStale(ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException ex) {
        return problem(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, "stale-version", "The resource was changed by someone else. Reload and retry.");
    }

    // Last resort: log everything, reveal nothing.
    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    ProblemDetail handleUnexpected(Exception ex) {
        log.error("Unhandled exception", ex);
        return problem(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR, "internal-error",
                "An unexpected error occurred. Quote the requestId when reporting it.");
    }

    // Also applied to every ProblemDetail Spring MVC builds itself.
    @Override
    protected ResponseEntity<Object> createResponseEntity(Object body, HttpHeaders headers,
                                                          HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {
        if (body instanceof ProblemDetail pd) enrich(pd, defaultCode(status));
        return super.createResponseEntity(body, headers, status, request);
    }

    private ProblemDetail problem(HttpStatusCode status, String code, String detail) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(status, detail);
        enrich(pd, code);
        return pd;
    }

    private void enrich(ProblemDetail pd, String code) {
        if (pd.getProperties() == null || !pd.getProperties().containsKey("code")) {
            pd.setType(URI.create(TYPE_BASE + code));
            pd.setProperty("code", code);
        }
        pd.setProperty("requestId", MDC.get(RequestIdFilter.MDC_KEY));
    }

    private static String defaultCode(HttpStatusCode status) {
        return switch (status.value()) {
            case 400 -> "bad-request";
            case 404 -> "not-found";
            case 405 -> "method-not-allowed";
            case 413 -> "file-too-large";
            case 415 -> "unsupported-media-type";
            default -> "http-" + status.value();
        };
    }
}
```

Security errors (`401`, `403`) happen in filters, *before* the controller advice can see them. Give the security config an `AuthenticationEntryPoint` and an `AccessDeniedHandler` that write the same JSON (use a shared `ProblemDetailWriter` that serializes a `ProblemDetail` to the response with `application/problem+json`).

Add the error codes to a single class (`ProblemCodes` with `public static final String` constants, or an enum) so codes aren't typed as free strings in 40 places, and keep it in sync with the catalogue in `docs/api-design.md` §3.

**Pitfalls:**

- Never put `ex.getMessage()` of an unexpected exception in the response: it can contain SQL, table names or data.
- A `DataIntegrityViolationException` from a unique constraint is often an *expected* conflict (duplicate file, duplicate active case). Don't map it globally to `409`: catch it where it happens, check which constraint fired, and throw a specific `ConflictException`. A global mapping would turn genuine bugs into misleading `409`s.
- Make sure 404s for unknown URLs also get ProblemDetail (in Boot 4 / Spring 7 the `NoResourceFoundException` path is handled by `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler`; verify with a test).

**Done when:** a `@WebMvcTest` slice with a dummy controller asserts `Content-Type: application/problem+json`, `type`, `code` and `requestId` for: a thrown `NotFoundException`, a validation failure (with `errors[]`), malformed JSON, an unknown URL, and a `RuntimeException` (body contains no exception message).

### 4.10 M2.11 — Request correlation ID

**What:** a filter that assigns each request an ID, puts it in the MDC and returns it in `X-Request-Id`.

**How:**

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.shared.web;

@Component
@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)   // before Spring Security, so even 401s carry the ID
public class RequestIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    public static final String HEADER = "X-Request-Id";
    public static final String MDC_KEY = "requestId";
    // Accept a caller's ID only if it's short and harmless (it ends up in logs).
    private static final Pattern SAFE = Pattern.compile("[A-Za-z0-9._-]{8,64}");

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, FilterChain chain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        String incoming = request.getHeader(HEADER);
        String requestId = (incoming != null && SAFE.matcher(incoming).matches())
                ? incoming
                : UUID.randomUUID().toString().replace("-", "").substring(0, 16);
        MDC.put(MDC_KEY, requestId);
        response.setHeader(HEADER, requestId);
        try {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            MDC.remove(MDC_KEY);   // threads are pooled: a leftover ID would label the next request
        }
    }
}
```

Show the ID in local (non-JSON) logs too, in `application-local.yml`:

```yaml
logging:
  pattern:
    correlation: "[%X{requestId:-}] "
```

The same request ID is written to `audit_event.request_id` (M4.3) by reading the MDC, so an audit row links back to its logs.

**Background jobs:** MDC is per thread (§2.8). When enqueueing a job (M5), store the request ID in the job payload; the worker puts it in the MDC while processing (and adds `jobId`), so logs from the worker can be traced to the upload that caused them.

**Pitfalls:**

- Validating the incoming header prevents log injection (a header containing newlines can forge log lines).
- Spring Boot with Micrometer Tracing adds `traceId`/`spanId` to the MDC automatically. If you add tracing later (M9), you can use the trace ID as the request ID; until then this filter is simpler and has no dependencies.

**Done when:** an integration test calls any URL, asserts the `X-Request-Id` header, asserts the same value is in the ProblemDetail's `requestId`, and asserts a valid incoming ID is echoed while `"bad\nvalue"` is replaced.

### 4.11 M2.12 — Structured JSON logging in `prod`

**What:** one JSON object per log line in production.

**Why:** log platforms (Loki, Elasticsearch, CloudWatch) index JSON fields, so you can query `requestId = "4f1c…"` or `level = ERROR AND logger = …ingestion…`. Multi-line stack traces in plain text get split into separate events; in JSON they're one field.

**How:** Spring Boot 3.4+ has structured logging built in; no Logback encoder dependency needed. `application-prod.yml` already contains:

```yaml
logging:
  structured:
    format:
      console: ecs        # Elastic Common Schema; alternatives: logstash, gelf
```

MDC entries (like `requestId`) are included as fields automatically. Add static fields that identify the node:

```yaml
logging:
  structured:
    ecs:
      service:
        name: reconflow
        environment: prod
```

(Verify the exact property names for service metadata in the Boot 4 logging docs.)

Keep **human-readable** logs in `local`: JSON is hard to read in a terminal.

**Pitfalls:** don't log request bodies or CSV rows. They contain references and amounts, which is financial data (M10.6 reviews this).

**Done when:** `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod,api DB_URL=… DB_USER=… DB_PASSWORD=… java -jar target/*.jar` prints JSON lines, and a request's lines contain `"requestId"`.

### 4.12 M2.16 — Base integration test class

**What:** one abstract class that every integration test extends.

**Why:** a single, shared Spring context and container for the whole test run (fast), and consistent setup (profile, MockMvc, helpers).

**How:** `src/test/java/com/reconflow/paymentreconflow/support/AbstractIntegrationTest.java`:

```java
package com.reconflow.paymentreconflow.support;

@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
@ActiveProfiles("test")
@Import(TestcontainersConfiguration.class)
public abstract class AbstractIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired protected MockMvc mockMvc;
    @Autowired protected JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    // Helpers added as the domain grows, e.g. in M3:
    //   protected TestOrg newOrganization() { ... }   // every test gets its own org
}
```

Update the existing test:

```java
class PaymentreconflowApplicationTests extends AbstractIntegrationTest {
    @Test
    void contextLoadsAndMigrationsApply() {
        Integer applied = jdbc.queryForObject(
                "select count(*) from flyway_schema_history where success", Integer.class);
        assertThat(applied).isGreaterThanOrEqualTo(3);
    }
}
```

**Test data isolation strategy (decide now, it affects every later test):**

| Strategy | Problem here |
|---|---|
| `TRUNCATE` all tables between tests | `audit_event` has a trigger that **forbids TRUNCATE** (V1). Don't fight it |
| `@Transactional` on tests (rollback after each) | Hides bugs: the code under test can't commit, so you can't test commit-time behaviour, multi-transaction flows, job workers or concurrency (M7.14) |
| **Each test creates its own organization** and only queries within it | **Chosen.** Works naturally with multi-tenancy (every query is org-scoped anyway), tests can't see each other's data, data accumulates harmlessly in a throwaway container |

So: never rely on a table being empty; always assert on data in *your* test's organization. Use `@Transactional` only for pure repository tests that don't need commits.

**Container reuse:** Spring's test context cache keeps one context (and so one container) for every test class with the same configuration. Avoid per-class `@MockitoBean`, `@TestPropertySource` or different `@ActiveProfiles` unless needed; each variation starts a new context and a new container. For faster local runs you can enable Testcontainers' reuse (`testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` in `~/.testcontainers.properties` and `.withReuse(true)`), never in CI.

**Naming convention:** unit tests `*Test.java` (Surefire, `test` phase); integration tests `*IT.java` (Failsafe, `integration-test` phase). Add the Failsafe plugin:

```xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <goals>
                <goal>integration-test</goal>
                <goal>verify</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
```

Then `./mvnw test` is fast (no Docker), and `./mvnw verify` runs everything. Rename the context test to `PaymentreconflowApplicationIT`.

**Done when:** two different `*IT` classes extending the base share one container (the log shows a single "Creating container for image: postgres:16.4-alpine").

### 4.13 M2.13 — Formatting with Spotless

**What:** an automatic formatter, checked in CI.

**Why:** see §2.9.

**How:** Palantir Java Format matches the `.editorconfig` from M0.6 (4-space indent, 120 columns):

```xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>com.diffplug.spotless</groupId>
    <artifactId>spotless-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version><!-- latest release; must support Java 25 syntax --></version>
    <configuration>
        <java>
            <palantirJavaFormat>
                <version><!-- a release that supports Java 25 --></version>
            </palantirJavaFormat>
            <removeUnusedImports/>
            <importOrder/>
            <trimTrailingWhitespace/>
            <endWithNewline/>
        </java>
        <pom>
            <sortPom>
                <expandEmptyElements>false</expandEmptyElements>
            </sortPom>
        </pom>
    </configuration>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <goals><goal>check</goal></goals>
            <phase>verify</phase>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
```

Workflow: `./mvnw spotless:apply` formats everything; `./mvnw verify` fails with a diff if anything is unformatted.

Make the **first** Spotless PR contain only the plugin plus the result of `spotless:apply`, with no other changes, so formatting churn doesn't hide real changes in review.

**Pitfalls:**

- Formatter versions must understand Java 25 syntax; an old version fails to parse newer constructs. Pin versions explicitly.
- Google Java Format (2-space indent) would contradict the `.editorconfig`; pick one style and make both agree.
- Don't format SQL migration files automatically: changing an applied migration's whitespace changes its checksum and Flyway refuses to start.

**Done when:** adding a badly indented line makes `./mvnw verify` fail; `./mvnw spotless:apply` fixes it.

### 4.14 M2.14 — Coverage with JaCoCo

**What:** a coverage report on every build, no threshold yet.

**Why:** visibility first. A threshold set before you know your baseline either blocks everything or means nothing. Look at the report, then decide in M9 whether to enforce something (e.g. ≥ 80% line coverage on `reconciliation` and `ingestion`).

**How:**

```xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
    <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version><!-- 0.8.14 or later: older versions can't instrument Java 25 class files --></version>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <id>prepare-agent</id>
            <goals><goal>prepare-agent</goal></goals>
        </execution>
        <execution>
            <id>prepare-agent-it</id>
            <goals><goal>prepare-agent-integration</goal></goals>
        </execution>
        <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals><goal>report</goal></goals>
        </execution>
        <execution>
            <id>report-it</id>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <goals><goal>report-integration</goal></goals>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
```

Reports: `target/site/jacoco/index.html` (unit) and `target/site/jacoco-it/index.html` (integration). A `merge` execution can combine them if you want one number.

**Pitfalls:** if Surefire/Failsafe have their own `<argLine>`, write it as `@{argLine} -Xmx1g` so JaCoCo's agent argument isn't overwritten. Coverage measures what ran, not what was checked: a test with no assertions gives 100% coverage.

**Done when:** `./mvnw verify` produces both reports.

### 4.15 M2.15 — GitHub Actions CI

**What:** a workflow that runs `./mvnw verify` on every PR and on pushes to `main`.

**How:** `.github/workflows/ci.yml`:

```yaml
name: CI

on:
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: [main]

# A newer push to the same PR cancels the older, still-running build.
concurrency:
  group: ci-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: true

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  build:
    name: build            # this exact name becomes the required status check
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 20
    steps:
      - name: Check out
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up JDK 25
        uses: actions/setup-java@v4
        with:
          distribution: temurin
          java-version: '25'
          cache: maven

      - name: Build, test, format check, coverage
        # -B: batch mode (no colour/progress noise). Testcontainers uses the
        # runner's Docker daemon; ubuntu-latest has it pre-installed.
        run: ./mvnw -B -ntp verify

      - name: Upload test reports on failure
        if: failure()
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: test-reports
          path: |
            target/surefire-reports/
            target/failsafe-reports/

      - name: Upload coverage report
        if: always()
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: coverage
          path: |
            target/site/jacoco/
            target/site/jacoco-it/
```

After the first successful run on a PR, **go back to the M0.4 branch protection** and turn on "Require status checks to pass", selecting `build`, with "Require branches to be up to date before merging". (Or via `gh api`, setting `required_status_checks` to `{"strict": true, "contexts": ["build"]}`; see the M0 plan §4.6.)

**Pitfalls:**

- `./mvnw` must be executable in Git (`git ls-files -s mvnw` shows mode `100755`). If not: `git update-index --chmod=+x mvnw`.
- No secrets are needed: tests use Testcontainers, not a shared database. Keep it that way; a CI that needs production-like secrets is a CI that leaks them.
- Pin action versions (at least to a major tag like `@v4`; full commit SHAs are stricter and recommended once you add dependency scanning in M10.5).

**Done when:** a PR shows a green `build` check; a PR with a failing test or unformatted code shows red and can't be merged.

### 4.16 Update the README

Replace the "Prepare the database" and "Test" sections:

- Prerequisites: Java 25 and Docker. PostgreSQL no longer needs installing.
- Run: `cp .env.example .env`, `docker compose up -d`, `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local,api,worker ./mvnw spring-boot:run`.
- Test: `./mvnw test` (unit, no Docker) and `./mvnw verify` (everything, Docker required).
- Fix "Flyway applies V1 and V2" → V1–V3.

---

## 5. Testing strategy

| Test | Type | Proves |
|---|---|---|
| `PaymentreconflowApplicationIT` | Integration (Testcontainers) | Context starts; Flyway applies V1–V3; Hibernate validation passes |
| `JpaSettingsTest` | Integration | `validate` and `open-in-view=false` stay set |
| `ModularityTest` | Unit (no Spring) | Module boundaries and allowed dependencies |
| `GlobalExceptionHandlerTest` | `@WebMvcTest` slice with a test-only controller | Every error path renders ProblemDetail with `type`, `code`, `requestId`; no leak of internal messages |
| `RequestIdFilterTest` | Unit (MockHttpServletRequest) + one integration assertion | Generation, echo of safe IDs, rejection of unsafe ones, MDC cleanup |
| `SecurityConfigIT` | Integration | Health is public; everything else `401` as ProblemDetail |
| `JsonConfigTest` | `@JsonTest` | `BigDecimal` serializes as a plain-notation string |
| `IdsTest` | Unit | `Ids.newId()` is version 7 (`uuid.version() == 7`) and monotonic across 10,000 calls |

Unit tests run in milliseconds and have no Docker; integration tests share one container. The full `./mvnw verify` should take well under two minutes at this stage. If it doesn't, look for extra Spring contexts in the log.

---

## 6. Exit criteria checklist

- [ ] `docker compose up -d` → `docker compose ps` shows `postgres` and `minio` healthy; the bucket exists
- [ ] `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=local,api,worker ./mvnw spring-boot:run` starts; log shows Flyway validated/applied `V1__initial_schema`, `V2__auth_hardening`, `V3__refresh_token_constraints`
- [ ] `curl -s localhost:8080/actuator/health` → `{"status":"UP"...}`
- [ ] `curl -si localhost:8080/api/v1/x` → `401`, `application/problem+json`, `X-Request-Id` header, `requestId` in body
- [ ] `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod,api` without `DB_URL` fails fast; with it, logs are JSON
- [ ] `./mvnw test` passes without Docker; `./mvnw verify` passes with Docker, including Spotless check and JaCoCo reports
- [ ] `ModularityTest` fails on a deliberate boundary violation (try it once, revert)
- [ ] GitHub Actions `build` is green on a PR and is a **required** check on `main`
- [ ] README setup updated; TASKS.md M2.1–M2.16 ticked; exit criterion text corrected to `V1__initial_schema.sql`

---

## 7. Interview questions this phase prepares you for

**Q: Who owns your database schema, Hibernate or migrations?**
Flyway owns it: versioned SQL scripts reviewed like code, applied in order, with checksums so an applied migration can't be silently changed. Hibernate runs with `ddl-auto=validate`, so a mismatch between an entity and its table fails the startup instead of a request. The schema uses partitioning, partial unique indexes and triggers that Hibernate couldn't generate anyway.

**Q: What does `spring.jpa.open-in-view` do and why disable it?**
It keeps the persistence context open for the whole request, so lazy associations load during JSON serialization: hidden N+1 queries, outside any transaction, holding a connection for the full request. Disabling it makes lazy loading outside a transaction fail loudly, which forces each service method to fetch exactly what it needs.

**Q: Why Testcontainers rather than H2?**
Tests should run on the same database engine as production. The schema relies on PostgreSQL features (partitioning, partial indexes, `NULLS NOT DISTINCT`, JSONB, triggers, and later `SKIP LOCKED` and row-level security) that H2 doesn't have or emulates differently. With `@ServiceConnection` the setup is a few lines, and one container is shared across the whole test run.

**Q: How do you keep tests isolated without truncating tables?**
Each test creates its own organization and only queries inside it, which matches how the multi-tenant application works anyway. Truncating would also break on the audit table, which forbids it by design. Rolling back each test in a transaction would hide commit-time behaviour and make concurrency tests impossible.

**Q: How do you trace one request through your logs?**
A filter assigns a request ID (or accepts a safe one from the caller), puts it in the logging MDC, and returns it in `X-Request-Id`; every log line and every error body includes it, and audit events store it. Because MDC is thread-local, background jobs carry the originating request ID in their payload and restore it in the worker.

**Q: How do you enforce architecture in a monolith?**
Each module is a top-level package; Spring Modulith treats sub-packages as internal and fails a test if another module uses them or if modules form a cycle. Allowed dependencies are declared per module, so even a legal-but-unplanned dependency fails. ArchUnit covers specific rules like "no `double` in money code".

**Q: What happens when you add Spring Security to a Spring Boot app?**
Everything becomes protected by default, including actuator endpoints, with HTTP Basic and a generated password. You define a `SecurityFilterChain` that states explicitly what's public (here only health and info) and denies everything else until real authentication exists. Deny-by-default is the safe direction to err in.

**Q: Why return errors as RFC 9457 Problem Details?**
It's a standard shape (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`) that clients and tools understand, with room for extension members. A stable `code` lets clients branch on errors without parsing English text, and `requestId` connects a user's error report to the server logs. A single handler also guarantees internal exception messages never leak.

---

## 8. Common mistakes

- **Adding a library instead of its Boot 4 starter**, then wondering why nothing auto-configures (Flyway was the first instance).
- **Floating image tags** (`postgres:latest`, `postgres:16`). A pinned tag makes "works today" mean "works next month".
- **A convenient default profile.** Production then inherits development settings when someone forgets `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE`.
- **`permitAll()` while waiting for M3.** Deny by default; open paths one at a time.
- **Mapping every `DataIntegrityViolationException` to 409 globally.** Real bugs then look like user conflicts.
- **Leaking exception messages** in the generic 500 handler.
- **Forgetting `MDC.remove`** in the filter: pooled threads carry the previous request's ID into the next one.
- **Many slightly different test configurations**, each starting a new Spring context and a new container. Builds get slow without anyone noticing why.
- **`TRUNCATE` in test cleanup.** The audit trigger rejects it, deliberately.
- **Formatting and logic in one PR.** Reviewers can't see the logic change in a 3,000-line whitespace diff.
- **Enabling a coverage threshold on day one.** Measure, then decide.
- **Turning on the required status check before the workflow has run once.** GitHub won't list a check it hasn't seen.
